A||N||16||Linx||low compression; new rings?||||5||
Z||000000||Linx||12-04-1999||10:59 PM||||Fellas,<BR>My '86SE smokes on the highway.  The compression is around 110 on all but #5, which is 90.  I squirted some oil in #5 and compression reached a whoppin' 150.  So- it's rings time.<BR>Autozone has the ring set for $30.  Cool.  But the guy asked me if I needed "standard size".  Huh?<BR>Nope- never done rings before.  I can replace the old ones with the same size, right? "standard size"?||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||12-04-1999||11:55 PM||||You say HUH to the term oversize but you are ready to buy the rings and slap them in. Doing rings is a major undertaking. You will also need to hone the cylinders or the new rings will never seat. It could also be a score on the cylinder wall. Then you will need to bore it and my require an oversize piston. I have three cylinders with low compression and it runs great. I could go on for ever but I wont. Just get more info before you start taking you motor apart. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-05-1999||12:00 AM||||Well...yes and no. First, you have to know for sure that the engine has never been rebuilt. If you are not the original owner, don't assume that the cylinders/pistons are standard.<P>If it is still a standard bore, then yes you could just replace the rings, BUT I don't recommend it. Usually when the rings are shot, the whole engine needs rebuilt. It's best to bore out the cylinders and install new pistons and rings all at the same time. The common oversize is 0.030" (30 thousands of an inch over).<P>Of course then the heads need rebuilt and valves ground. Cam and timing gear set will need replaced. The crank needs checked and possibly ground. Obviously you'll need new rod/main bearings, oil pump, and complete gasket set. The list goes on and on.<P>I'm not trying to put you down, but if you're asking these types of questions, you're in way over your head. The engines needs pulled completely out of the car and totally disassembled to do this right.<P>Engine rebuilding requires a number of special tools and you'll still need to take lots of stuff into the machine shop, even if you're doing it yourself. Rebuilding a engine is not cheap either.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||12-05-1999||12:08 AM||||Tombo- Im there already; I'll be taking the pistons out tomorrow.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/linxramblr/block.jpg">  <BR>I know I gotta hone the cylinders, and remove the ridge, too.  I aint a complete boob- hell, i replaced the crank this time last year (300 miles ago). oh, and the timing gears and oil pump and...man, i cant remember what else.  Know what?  I did it all with the engine <I>in</I>.  I dont have a lift.<P>I was just a little fuzzy on the topic of ring sizing.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 12-05-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phaeton||12-05-1999||12:19 AM||||Keep the Hayne's manual but go to your local chevrolet dealer and get a copy Chevrolet Power.  The factory prints them out and include all the clearances, hone specs, mesurements, etc. for street vehicles.  Its very weak on step by step procedures, but ring end gaps and piston side clearances are all in there.||||reg||7
Z||000005||batboy||12-05-1999||01:20 AM||||Well Linx, you amaze me!!! When you get the pistons out and after you clean them up, check the tops of the pistons. Oversized ones will have a number stamped on it (such as 030). Standard pistons either won't have anything on them or will say STD. You must match the rings to the pistons. If the pistons are standard size, then you must use standard rings, etc. Good luck. It must be a b!tch working on the firewall side of the engine. Also, how are you going to get the inside of the engine clean after honing the cylinders?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Linx||12-05-1999||02:38 AM||||Ahhh...so it's mostly about reboring, if necessary.  I see.  BatBoy; now it becomes clearer.  So, supposing that after honing, the diameter of the cylinder remains within a spec, and the piston is too, I can use the <I>standard ring</I>.<P>Where removing the minimum of metal is concerned, how do i clean the carbon build up on the piston and the head/valves?  And the old head gasket material?  This is gonna be elbow grease, eh?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||12-05-1999||02:49 AM||||<BR>Linx, can't you rent a hoist? <P>I can't imagine trying to rebuild an engine <B>properly</B> while it's still in the vehicle. It's insanity!||||reg||5
Z||000008||batboy||12-05-1999||03:14 AM||||Yes, you got it now. If you don't have access to a bore micrometer, the next best way to check the specs is to put a new ring about a 1/4 of the way down in the cylinder. It must be square. You can use a cleaned up piston with the old rings removed and reversed (up end down) to push the ring squarely down into the bore. Then carefully measure the end gap with feeler gauges.<P>The elbow grease method is the best way to clean parts, just don't scratch the metal. The cast iron block and heads are fairly tough, You can carefully use a wire brush and a wide flat scraper (or putty knife) on them. It's the aluminum alloy pistons you have to be very cautious with. Don't scratch up the sides (piston skirts). The ring landing have to be very carefully cleaned of carbon deposits. They do make a special tool for this job that doesn't cost much. A little cleaning solvent might help out.<P>I'd recommend taking the heads into the shop and have them rebuilt, unless that was done very recently. Lots of power is gained or lost in the heads. Worn valve guides and valve seals are one of the top reasons engines burn oil, right up there with worn piston rings. You should have the valves and seats reground or else you won't make good compression. You have to buy a gasket set anyway so at least put the valve seals in.<P>Well, maybe you already knew most of this stuff, but I typed it all up anyway, maybe someone else reading this will learn something. Oh yeah, you do know about having to use a ring compressor to reinstall the pistons, right. Just trying to look out for you Fiero lovers.<P>I just reread your last post. If you reuse the old pistons and they are standard size, you HAVE to use standard size rings or else you'll have no end gap. I'm still not sure how you're going to keep crap from going down into the water jacket and into the bottom end of the block.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-05-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Dan||12-05-1999||10:00 AM|||| Want some really good advice?Go ahead and pull the motor and do a proper rebuild.If the motor isn't professionally line bored <BR>you may well end up with an engine that you may be very dissatisified with.Years ago when I was in the Navy I rebuilt an engine at the hobby shop.So were several other people.One guy was building up his small block chevy.He spent about $2800 including<BR>cam,lifters,oversized pistons,line boring,<BR>crank, rods,ported heads,high rise manifold,etc. Anyway,when he started it up,it blew up and found that he forgot to replace the cam bearings.Cylinders never wear uniformily.They wear eggshaped around the bore and more at the top than at the bottom.I would guess to say that when you replaCED THE crank and bearings you recovered oil pressure that allowed it yo blow passed the bad rings.You might seriously consider a used engine while you take your time on your baby.I like a reconditioned engine that I know is done right to preserve and increase horsepower and reliability, and drive the hell out of it.When you get your old piston out, take the top compression ring out and measure it at the top and bottom of the bore.Chances are if the gap is within tolerances at the bottom, it won't be at the top,hence,your compression will still be low and all that hard work for naught.If you oversize only one piston,you will experience power loss over doing a complete rebuild.In any case, good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mutfish||12-05-1999||11:03 AM||||I agree with dan,I've built quite a few motors and trust me it's hard enough to get a reliable good running one when you do it by the book. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Linx||12-05-1999||01:53 PM||||Man oh man!  Ya'll are havin' me think "what the heck have I got myself into!"<BR>I'm fortunate enought that time and money arent a problem here...so I think I <I>will</I> take it out and take it somewhere.  It should be easier...heck, it's all apart now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Man- it'd be great to sit down with you fellas, have a few beers and ask questions...like, why does the bore wear egg shaped?  Why cant I use the same headbolts?  Whose idea was it to make it so you cant pull a piston out from the bottom instead of the top???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks, guys.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dan||12-06-1999||04:37 AM|||| It wears eggshaped because of two factors. One: You have localized hotspots in your cylinder due to the design of your cooling water jacket.<BR>Two: The friction and inertia forces within the cylinder and piston.It is not unusual for many designs of blocks to wear out a particular cylinder first. Many race engine builders have their motors cryogenically treated to help relieve internal stresses caused during the pouring of the block and curing.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eddie||12-07-1999||08:31 PM||||Batboy whats up I have a ? for you about your post on the valves when my motor was being redone the guy opened the valve ports is that bad it was alot of extra money.Ill kill that guy if so .||||reg||11
Z||000014||batboy||12-07-1999||09:21 PM||||Whoa! I'm going to have a possible murder on my conscious if I say the wrong thing here. **haha** Tell me exactly what the guy did. If it's a process called pocket porting and <B>IF</B> it was done correctly, that increases flow which adds power. Possibly up to about 10 extra ponies on a 2.8 depending on whether you're running headers to take advantage of this extra flow in the heads.||||reg||1
